# Vocabulary (optional but strongly encouraged)

You may include a `<vocabulary>` block **after** the `<features>`
block, at the top level of your output — at the same nesting as
`<features>`, not inside it. Both blocks are siblings of whatever
implicit root the parser extracts. The vocabulary block flags terms
the project uses in a project-specific sense — anything where a
generic LLM reading the term in isolation would get the meaning
subtly wrong. Flag a term whenever the input doc genuinely has
project-specific terminology; if the project is too generic for any
term to warrant flagging, omit the block rather than padding it with
filler (the user can always add vocabulary entries later). The
defining work — writing the definition, the disambiguation, and any
see-also cross-references — happens downstream, in vocab's own pass;
here you only mint the candidate.

The grammar:

    <vocabulary>
      <term name="tranche" scope="feature" feature-name="Billing"/>
      <term name="session" scope="project"/>
    </vocabulary>

# Vocabulary rules

* `<vocabulary>` is optional. If you include it, it comes after
  `<features>` in the output, as a sibling block, and must contain at
  least one `<term>` entry.
* Each `<term>` is a **self-closing** element carrying only
  attributes — no children, no `<vocab-entry>`, no `<definition>`, no
  `<disambiguation>`, no `<see-also>`. That elaboration is vocab's
  job, not this tier's.
* Each `<term>` has a `name` attribute (the term being flagged) and a
  `scope` attribute that is exactly `"project"` or `"feature"`.
  * `scope="project"` means the term is relevant project-wide and
    should be in every regen prompt context at every tier.
  * `scope="feature"` means the term is specific to one feature's
    subtree. It also requires a `feature-name` attribute whose value
    matches an exact feature name in the same `<features>` block.
* Term names must be unique within a scope. Two project-level terms
  cannot share a name. Two feature-local terms within the same
  feature cannot share a name. A project-level term and a
  feature-local term *can* share a name — scope disambiguates them.
* Scan the input doc for terms the user uses in a project-specific
  sense — "boulder," "tranche," "widget," or any phrase that seems
  loaded with meaning particular to the project — and flag them as
  vocabulary entries. Guideline: *if a generic LLM reading the term in
  isolation would get it subtly wrong, flag it for vocabulary*.
* Vocabulary entries are not features. Don't confuse the two: features
  describe *what the project does*; vocabulary flags *what words the
  project uses* — the meaning itself gets written up later,
  downstream.
